{"page":154,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.20386,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"140 Chapter 3: Derivatives"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.093,"w":0.27891,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Derivatives of Exponential Functions"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.11766,"w":0.59308,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"We briefly reviewed exponential functions in Section 1.5. When we apply the definition of the derivative to ƒ(x) = ax, we get"},{"x":0.54961,"y":0.15045,"w":0.0658,"h":0.01793,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ax+h - ax"},{"x":0.4546,"y":0.15559,"w":0.09205,"h":0.02729,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"d dx (ax) = lim"},{"x":0.57842,"y":0.16472,"w":0.2095,"h":0.01815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"h Derivative definition"},{"x":0.52353,"y":0.17255,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.54974,"y":0.18025,"w":0.07541,"h":0.02305,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ax # ah - ax"},{"x":0.58336,"y":0.19141,"w":0.17016,"h":0.02637,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"h ax+h = ax # ah"},{"x":0.50667,"y":0.19704,"w":0.04011,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= lim"},{"x":0.52367,"y":0.20746,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.52367,"y":0.22028,"w":0.09505,"h":0.03249,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"hS0ax # ah - 1"},{"x":0.50668,"y":0.23195,"w":0.04011,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= lim"},{"x":0.59146,"y":0.2334,"w":0.17316,"h":0.0193,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"h Factoring out ax"},{"x":0.57647,"y":0.25455,"w":0.04633,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ah - 1"},{"x":0.50667,"y":0.25663,"w":0.06418,"h":0.02306,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= ax # lim"},{"x":0.59554,"y":0.2675,"w":0.20993,"h":0.01946,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"h ax is constant as h S 0."},{"x":0.54774,"y":0.27665,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.56292,"y":0.28947,"w":0.04633,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ah - 1"},{"x":0.45583,"y":0.29138,"w":0.45642,"h":0.03986,"fs":0.01918,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"h b # ax. (1) (1+)1+*"},{"x":0.50667,"y":0.29138,"w":0.05062,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= alim"},{"x":0.53418,"y":0.31156,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.52925,"y":0.33185,"w":0.08932,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"a fixed number L"},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.35155,"w":0.59302,"h":0.04491,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Thus we see that the derivative of ax is a constant multiple L of ax. The constant L is a limit unlike any we have encountered before. Note, however, that it equals the derivative of ƒ(x) = ax at x = 0:"},{"x":0.16487,"y":0.39978,"w":0.12025,"h":0.01286,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"a = 3 a = 2.5"},{"x":0.20291,"y":0.39979,"w":0.0298,"h":0.01076,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"a = e"},{"x":0.56409,"y":0.40111,"w":0.05196,"h":0.01793,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ah - a0"},{"x":0.67064,"y":0.40111,"w":0.04634,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ah - 1"},{"x":0.48098,"y":0.41279,"w":0.08014,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ′(0) = lim"},{"x":0.58598,"y":0.41279,"w":0.0817,"h":0.02074,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"h = lim"},{"x":0.68971,"y":0.41279,"w":0.06489,"h":0.02074,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"h = L."},{"x":0.53801,"y":0.42321,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.64456,"y":0.42321,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.44273,"w":0.59302,"h":0.06026,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"The limit L is therefore the slope of the graph of ƒ(x) = ax where it crosses the y-axis. In Chapter 7, where we carefully develop the logarithmic and exponential functions, we prove that the limit L exists and has the value ln a. For now we investigate values of L by graphing the function y = (ah - 1)>h and studying its behavior as h approaches 0."},{"x":0.25656,"y":0.45601,"w":0.03053,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"a = 2"},{"x":0.1021,"y":0.45834,"w":0.01636,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"1.1"},{"x":0.04998,"y":0.46107,"w":0.04147,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"L = 1.0"},{"x":0.12683,"y":0.48184,"w":0.0229,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"0.92"},{"x":0.12683,"y":0.49233,"w":0.14362,"h":0.01946,"fs":0.01023,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"0.69 y = , a > 0 ah −1"},{"x":0.32331,"y":0.49511,"w":0.59303,"h":0.08462,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"Figure 3.12 shows the graphs of y = (ah - 1)>h for four different values of a. The limit L is approximately 0.69 if a = 2, about 0.92 if a = 2.5, and about 1.1 if a = 3. It appears that the value of L is 1 at some number a chosen between 2.5 and 3. That number is given by a = e ≈2.718281828. With this choice of base we obtain the natural expo- nential function ƒ(x) = ex as in Section 1.5, and see that it satisfies the property"},{"x":0.04848,"y":0.58226,"w":0.25477,"h":0.08902,"fs":0.01151,"n":7,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 3.12 The position of the curve y = (ah - 1)>h, a 7 0, varies continu- ously with a. The limit L of y as h S 0 changes with different values of a. The number for which L = 1 as h S 0 is the number e between a = 2 and a = 3."},{"x":0.62184,"y":0.58438,"w":0.04523,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"eh - 1"},{"x":0.5359,"y":0.59605,"w":0.08155,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ′(0) = lim"},{"x":0.64036,"y":0.59605,"w":0.27188,"h":0.02074,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"h = 1 (2)"},{"x":0.59293,"y":0.60648,"w":0.02453,"h":0.01039,"fs":0.00895,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.62741,"w":0.5889,"h":0.0435,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"because it is the exponential function whose graph has slope 1 when it crosses the y-axis. That the limit is 1 implies an important relationship between the natural exponential func- tion ex and its derivative:"},{"x":0.53995,"y":0.65976,"w":0.08404,"h":0.04958,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"hS0 ¢eh - 1"},{"x":0.59728,"y":0.65976,"w":0.19188,"h":0.0495,"fs":0.01918,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"h ≤# ex Eq. (1) with a = e"},{"x":0.47309,"y":0.68198,"w":0.09139,"h":0.02729,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"d dx (ex) = lim"},{"x":0.52296,"y":0.70706,"w":0.20364,"h":0.02238,"fs":0.01918,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"= 1 # ex = ex. Eq. (2)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.73908,"w":0.41541,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Therefore the natural exponential function is its own derivative."},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.78227,"w":0.33034,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Derivative of the Natural Exponential Function"},{"x":0.57836,"y":0.80954,"w":0.07894,"h":0.02729,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"d dx (ex) = ex"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.86319,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02984,"fs":0.01407,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 5 Find an equation for a line that is tangent to the graph of y = ex and goes through the origin."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.90625,"w":0.59302,"h":0.04434,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Solution Since the line passes through the origin, its equation is of the form y = mx, where m is the slope. If it is tangent to the graph at the point (a, ea), the slope is m = (ea - 0)>(a - 0). The slope of the natural exponential at x = a is ea. Because these"}]}